# Expectation record for 07_variadics/005_va_list_forwarding.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 005_va_list_forwarding
area               = 07_variadics
description        = An argument list forwarded to a second variadic-consuming function, once chained
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Once the callee that received the list has invoked va_arg the list is indeterminate in the
forwarding frame, so nothing is read again after a forward: the wrappers contain no va_arg
at all and the owning frame only performs its required va_end.  A v-style consumer never
starts, copies or ends the list it receives, never assigns to that parameter and never
takes its address, because va_end must be invoked by the frame that invoked va_start.  No
list is read past its argument count.  <stdarg.h> is included because a variadic function
cannot be written without it and it is in both compilers' freestanding sets; printf is still
hand-declared, because bcc ships no stdio.h.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
